WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.953
NARRATOR: Every oscillating mechanical system
has ways it naturally prefers to move.

2
00:00:04.953 --> 00:00:09.768
NARRATOR: Push a swing slowly, or far too
quickly, and the motion stays modest.

3
00:00:09.768 --> 00:00:14.942
NARRATOR: Push near its natural rhythm and
each input can reinforce the motion already

4
00:00:14.942 --> 00:00:15.312
underway.

5
00:00:15.312 --> 00:00:19.604
NARRATOR: That frequency-sensitive
amplification is the core idea behind

6
00:00:19.604 --> 00:00:20.081
resonance.

7
00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:25.121
NARRATOR: A frequency-response plot puts
driving frequency on the horizontal axis and

8
00:00:25.121 --> 00:00:27.589
response amplitude on the vertical axis.

9
00:00:27.589 --> 00:00:29.707
NARRATOR: The peak marks the resonant region.

10
00:00:29.707 --> 00:00:34.180
NARRATOR: Its location and width tell
engineers which excitations are most dangerous

11
00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.298
or most useful.

12
00:00:35.298 --> 00:00:40.883
NARRATOR: A design does not merely ask whether
a force exists; it asks how often that force

13
00:00:40.883 --> 00:00:41.212
repeats.

14
00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:46.111
NARRATOR: Real systems lose energy through
damping, so their response does not grow

15
00:00:46.111 --> 00:00:46.847
without bound.

16
00:00:46.847 --> 00:00:52.010
NARRATOR: Engineers can add damping, change
stiffness or mass, or keep operating speeds

17
00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.393
away from a troublesome natural frequency.

18
00:00:54.393 --> 00:00:59.025
NARRATOR: The practical lesson is simple:
resonance is not automatically failure, but

19
00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:04.428
ignoring the frequency relationship can turn a
small repeating force into a large motion.
